  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nWidespread cloud coverage is being observed this morning associated \nwith moisture from Tropical Cyclone Marie. Light to locally moderate \nshowers have been observed across much of the area through the \nmorning, with the bulk of the rain at 11 AM north of San Diego \nCounty. There remains a 5 to 10 percent chance of thunderstorms over \nthe San Bernardino Mountains and High Desert this afternoon. Current \nforecast has an additional 0.30 to 0.50 inches of rain accumulating \nover the San Bernardino Mountains, with an additional 0.05 to 0.15 \ninches elsewhere north of San Diego County. Less than 0.05 inches of \nadditional rain are expected for San Diego County. \n\nLingering moisture tomorrow and Tuesday will continue chances of \nprecipitation, mainly over the mountains. Afternoon shower and \npotentially thunderstorm chances will continue into Thursday. A \nridge of high pressure will build from the south for the middle of \nthe week which will turn the upper level flow southeasterly. \nCurrently Wednesday looks like the most likely day for showers and \nthunderstorms to develop over the mountains in the afternoon. Low \nconfidence in details surrounding rainfall rates at this time. \nShower and thunderstorm chances have decreased for Thursday, with \nthe potential for the ridge of high pressure moving right over \nSouthern California, which would result in a very stable air mass\naloft.\n\nHigh temperatures Monday are expected to be 5 to 10 degrees \nwarmer than today, with less cloud coverage expected. High \ntemperatures will continue to increase into Wednesday for areas \nwest of the mountains and Thursday for the deserts. Above average \nhigh and low temperatures can be expected for the majority of this\nweek. Widespread minor to moderate HeatRisk is expected for \ninland areas Tuesday through Thursday.",
